# Answer 4 · Short Note: Government Initiatives for Combating Violence against Women (CSS 2018)
@py question 2018-8c

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map for the short note: every branch is one headed point.
@note A 10-mark note: set out the framework, the laws, the institutions, the services and the courts the state has created, then assess them against the enforcement gap.
@ask Short note: GOVERNMENT INITIATIVES for COMBATING VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN
(1) Framework: Constitution, CEDAW, SDG 5.2
(2) Laws: 2006, 2010, 2011, provincial DV laws, 2016, 2021
(3) Institutions: NCSW, women's police stations, ombudspersons
(4) Services and courts: VAWC Multan, helplines, shelters, special courts
(5) Assessment: strong on paper, weak in enforcement
:::

## Decoding the question
A **short note of 10 marks**: about **550–650 words**. "**Government initiatives**" means what the **state** has done — laws, policies, institutions, services and courts — not what NGOs have done. Organise them clearly and end with a short **assessment**.

## The model answer

### 1. The framework
The **Constitution** guarantees security of person (**Article 9**), dignity (**Article 14**) and equality, with special provision for women (**Article 25**). Pakistan acceded to **CEDAW** in ==1996==, whose General Recommendations 19 and 35 treat gender-based violence as discrimination, and it has committed to **SDG target 5.2** — the elimination of all violence against women and girls. Its **GSP+** trade status (==2014==) also depends on implementing human-rights conventions. The **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==) committed the state to eliminating violence against women.

### 2. Laws
Since 2006, the state has built a substantial legal framework:
- the **Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act 2006**, which moved rape out of the Zina Ordinance and back into the Penal Code;
- the **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act 2010** (amended ==2022==) and **Section 509 PPC** on public harassment;
- the **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011** and the **Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act 2011**, which criminalised *vani*, *swara*, forced marriage and denial of inheritance;
- provincial **domestic-violence laws** — Sindh (==2013==), Balochistan (==2014==), Punjab (==2016==), Khyber Pakhtunkhwa (==2021==) and Islamabad (==2025==);
- the ==2016== laws on **honour killing** (life imprisonment even after forgiveness) and **rape** (DNA testing), and the **Prevention of Electronic Crimes Act 2016**;
- the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021**, with special investigation units and special courts.

### 3. Institutions
- The **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==; statutory since ==2012==) and provincial commissions review laws and policies.
- **Women's police stations** were created in ==1994==, and many police stations now have women's help desks.
- Federal and provincial **Ombudspersons** hear workplace-harassment complaints, and an ombudsperson handles women's property rights under the ==2020== law.
- The **Punjab Women Protection Authority** (==2017==) runs the protection system under the Punjab Act, including **District Women Protection Committees** and **Women Protection Officers**.
- The **Ministry of Human Rights** (==2015==) co-ordinates federal policy and reporting to UN treaty bodies.

::: fig grid | Government initiatives against violence against women.
@cols 2
Laws: 2006, 2010, 2011, DV laws 2013–2025, 2016, 2021
Institutions: NCSW, women's police stations, ombudspersons, PWPA
Services: VAWC Multan, helpline 1043, Dar-ul-Aman shelters
Justice: special courts and investigation units (2021); GBV courts
:::

### 4. Services and courts
- The **Violence against Women Centre** in **Multan** (==2017==), set up under the **Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act 2016**, is a one-stop centre for FIRs, medical examination, forensic evidence, legal aid and counselling.
- **Helplines** such as the Punjab Women Helpline **1043** and the federal **1099** human-rights helpline provide advice and rescue.
- ***Dar-ul-Aman*** shelters run by provincial social welfare departments give temporary refuge, although many are overcrowded and restrict residents' freedom.
- **Special courts** and **Special Sexual Offences Investigation Units** under the 2021 Act, and gender-based violence courts piloted in Punjab, aim to speed up trials and improve convictions.
- The **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==), paid to women, strengthens their economic position at home.
- The **Zainab Alert, Response and Recovery Act 2020** created a system for rapid response to missing and abused children.

### 5. Assessment
The state's initiatives are **impressive on paper** and have made violence a public, not private, matter: Pakistan now has laws against almost every major form of violence and a growing network of services. But **enforcement lags**. In ==2024==, conviction rates were about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing (SSDO); police often refuse to register complaints; families force settlements; one-stop centres and shelters are too few and under-funded; and provincial capacity varies after the **18th Amendment** (==2010==). Many women — **56%** of those who suffered violence, according to the PDHS 2017–18 — never seek help at all. Laws such as the Punjab Act were also attacked as "un-Islamic" by religious parties, which slowed implementation. The priority now is not more initiatives but making existing ones **work**: centres in every district, trained police and prosecutors, faster courts, witness protection and public awareness.

## Why this answer scores
- Organises initiatives into **framework, laws, institutions, services and courts**.
- Names each law and institution with its **year** — the facts examiners check.
- Ends with a balanced **assessment** backed by data on the enforcement gap.
- A **grid figure** gives the examiner the whole picture at a glance.
